---
// firma2-project-comments — the notes ON this record: the running account of
// coordination around a project that a program manager keeps beside the numbers
// instead of in an inbox. The third tab of the project's record rail.
//
// THE ONE SECTION ON THIS PAGE WITH NO NUMBER IN IT, and that is what earns it
// the rail. Everything else the page carries is either a tracked series or a
// stored scalar; a comment is neither — it is the reason a number looks the way
// it does. "Reported values stay provisional until the survey crew closes out"
// is not visible anywhere in the measures table, and a reader who has just read
// that table is exactly the reader who needs it. So it sits one tab away from
// the facts rather than a screen below the map.
//
// NEWEST FIRST. A comment thread is read to find out WHAT CHANGED, not to
// reconstruct a history, so the most recent note is the one that gets the top
// of the panel. This inverts the milestone timeline directly below it in the
// main column, which runs oldest-first — deliberately: a timeline is a shape
// you read along, a thread is a stack you read the top of.
//
// AUTHOR AND DATE SHARE A LINE, pushed apart. They are the two things a reader
// scans a thread by ("who said it" / "how stale is it") and neither is the
// note, so they ride above it on one row rather than each taking a line of a
// 22rem column. The `.repel` that arranges them belongs to firma2-contact-card
// (the date goes in its `trailing` slot) rather than to this file — see the note
// at the call site for why the card, not the thread, has to own that row.
//
// AND THE AUTHOR IS A CONTACT CARD, not a name. Every commenter is one of the
// record's own contacts, so the thread renders the same firma2-contact-card the
// Contacts tab renders: hover or focus a name and you get their role, their
// organization and their address without the thread having to print any of it.
// That is what let a whole line come out of every note — the organization used
// to sit under the name here — and it is the reason the card is a shared
// component rather than markup inside the contacts panel.
//
// AN EMPTY THREAD IS A REAL STATE, and a quarter of the portfolio is in it (the
// mock derives the count from the project's own name — see
// firma2-project-detail). That is on purpose: an empty state nobody can reach
// is an empty state nobody reviews. It says what is absent and nothing else —
// the recovery action is the button underneath it, so the empty state does not
// also describe it.
//
// bcn-lego-checked: no esa- comment/thread/activity/feed lego — walked the
// catalog (`ls node_modules/@esa/ecology/src/components/`, 67 files): the
// closest are esa-alert-box (a message ABOUT the page, addressed to the reader,
// not a record of who said what) and esa-card (the main column's section
// surface, which the rail already supplies). Beacon is not cloned on this
// machine. Everything else IS a lego or a component: firma2-rail-section is the
// panel shell, firma2-contact-card is the author, esa-empty-state is the empty
// thread, esa-button is the Add affordance, and the layout is `.stack` +
// `.repel` with no bespoke flex anywhere.
import Firma2RailSection from '../shared/firma2-rail-section.astro';
import Firma2ContactCard from '../shared/firma2-contact-card.astro';
import EsaEmptyState from '@esa/ecology/esa-empty-state.astro';
import EsaButton from '@esa/ecology/esa-button.astro';
import type { ProjectComment } from '../../data/firma2-project-detail';

interface Props {
  /** Newest first; may be empty. */
  comments: ProjectComment[];
}

const { comments } = Astro.props;
---
<!-- TITLE HIDDEN: the tab above already says "Comments". See the prop's note in
     firma2-rail-section for why the heading stays in the document. -->
<Firma2RailSection title="Comments" titleHidden>
  <div class="stack" data-gap="lg">
    {comments.length === 0 ? (
      <!-- `sm`, because this empty state lives in a 22rem column rather than in
           a full-width table: the md rung's type and padding are sized for a
           page-wide well. No description — the Add button below IS the recovery
           action, and an empty state that also spells it out is the same
           instruction twice. -->
      <EsaEmptyState size="sm" title="No comments yet" />
    ) : (
      <ol class="firma2-project-comments stack" data-gap="lg">
        {comments.map((comment) => (
          <li class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
            {/* THE AUTHOR IS THE SAME OBJECT THE CONTACTS TAB RENDERS — the
                whole reason firma2-contact-card is a shared component and the
                reason a comment carries its author as a CONTACT rather than as a
                copied name. It also deletes a line from every note: the
                commenter's organization used to print under their name here, and
                it now lives in the card, one hover away, where it is available
                without costing the thread a line per comment.

                THE DATE RIDES IN THE CARD'S `trailing` SLOT rather than beside
                it in a `.repel` of this file's own. The card's trigger has to
                span the row — esa-popover centres its panel on the trigger and
                the rail clips anything that leaves the column, so a trigger only
                as wide as a name puts the card half outside it — and two things
                cannot both span one row. The card repels its own byline
                instead, which lands the date in the same place with none of the
                geometry split across two files. */}
            <Firma2ContactCard contact={comment.author} nameClass="typography-label-sm-strong">
              {/* A <time> would be the right element and this mock has no
                  machine date to put in its datetime — the record stores the
                  rendered string, for the determinism reason firma2-project-
                  detail states. A <time> with no datetime is just a span that
                  claims to be more, so it is a span. */}
              <span slot="trailing" class="firma2-project-comments__date typography-body-xs">
                {comment.date}
              </span>
            </Firma2ContactCard>
            <p class="firma2-project-comments__body typography-body-sm">{comment.body}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    )}

    <!-- INERT, AND DELIBERATELY NOT `disabled` — the same stance every Add
         affordance on this page takes. "Add comment" rather than "Comment":
         the verb form matches its four siblings in the main column (Add
         measure, Add source, Add milestone, Add area), and a rail that used a
         different grammar for the same act would read as a different kind of
         control. -->
    <div>
      <EsaButton variant="chrome" size="sm" icon="plus">Add comment</EsaButton>
    </div>
  </div>
</Firma2RailSection>

<style>
  /* An <ol> in a rail: the ordinals carry nothing a reader wants (a comment is
     identified by who and when, not by its number) but the ORDER is real, so
     the semantics stay and only the marker goes.

     The inline padding goes with it, and it has to be said explicitly:
     layouts.css zeroes the UA MARGIN on ul/ol at :where() but not the 40px
     marker gutter, so a list that only turns off `list-style` still sits
     indented — which put every comment two thirds of an inch right of the Add
     button under it and of the labels in the tab beside it. Three tabs sharing
     one column have to share one left edge. */
  .firma2-project-comments {
    list-style: none;

    /* THE UA'S 40px MARKER GUTTER IS REPLACED, NOT ZEROED — and the value it is
       replaced with is the one the other two tabs stand on. layouts.css zeroes
       the UA margin on ul/ol at :where() but not that gutter, so a list that
       only turns off `list-style` still sits two thirds of an inch right of
       everything around it.

       Zero would be the obvious replacement and it is the wrong one. Key facts
       and Contacts both render their values inside firma2-editable-field's quiet
       field box, whose text is inset by the md control's own inline padding plus
       its border — so a flush comment list puts the third tab's content 13px
       left of the first two, and switching tabs jogs the whole column sideways.
       Three tabs share one column; they share its left edge. Same expression as
       the field's own `__head` rule, restated for the same reason it is restated
       in firma2-project-contacts: the two must not drift. */
    padding-inline-start: calc(var(--spacing-300, 0.75rem) + var(--form-border-width, 1px));
  }

  .firma2-project-comments__date {
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary, #646464);
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* The date must not wrap mid-string when the author's name is long — `.repel`
     wraps the PAIR, which is the behaviour we want, and this keeps the date
     whole when it does. */
  .firma2-project-comments__date {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .firma2-project-comments__body {
    margin: 0;
    /* Primary ink: the note is the content of this panel, and the two lines
       above it are its metadata. Reversing that — a grey note under a black
       name — is the read every comment thread that looks like a changelog got
       wrong. */
    color: var(--color-content-default, #202020);
  }
</style>
